# Packaging the website

The website is a small web application: a page the browser loads, a client that boots it, and a server that renders it first for a reader and a crawler. Vite bundles the client; the server runs under Bun. This chapter is the files around the code — the page, the manifest, the TypeScript settings, the build, and the small program that keeps the build and the server running while the site is developed.

## The page {Tangle}[index.html]

The page is the shell the application fills. It carries an empty `#root` for the view to mount into, and the module that boots the client.

The site is set in two families. Space Grotesk carries the headings, and Lilex carries everything else — the running text as well as the code, since the whole site is set in mono. Lilex has no Google entry, so it arrives as three `@font-face` rules pointing at its own files, with IBM Plex Mono standing behind it in the stack for the moment before they land. Noto Color Emoji comes along for the emoji the documentation shows.

The page's icon in a browser tab is Loom's language emblem, the illuminated L among the logo's flowers. It is the emblem's light face, `loom.svg`, on its navy tile: the tile carries its own ground, so it reads on a light tab bar and a dark one alike, and its sixteen-cell grid lands one cell to a pixel at a tab's sixteen pixels. A phone saving the site to its home screen wants a picture rather than a drawing, so `apple-touch-icon.png` is the same emblem at a hundred and eighty pixels, eleven to a cell inside a two-pixel navy border.

A link to the site shared in a chat or a feed shows a preview card, and the card is drawn from tags in the head. The Open Graph tags give the card its title, its line of description and its picture; the Twitter tag asks for the large picture rather than a thumbnail. The picture, `og.png`, is the site's own landing in miniature, at the twelve hundred by six hundred and thirty pixels every network crops to: the mark at rest, its black pill and its open eyes, beside the wordmark, with the site's heading under them. A preview is a still picture among many in a feed, so it shows the mark the way a reader first meets it, before any carpet is woven across it. The card already names the site's address under the picture, so the picture leaves it out. Every route shares the one card, since a link anywhere on the site is a link to Loom.

The server rewrites three spots: the `#root` it fills with the rendered body, the head where it inlines the model, and the `<title>` it replaces with the route's own. The first two it finds by exact text, so the strings `<div id="root"></div>` and `</head>` matter as much as the markup; the title it finds by its element, so the words below are a default no reader sees.

=>

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>loom — programs written to be read</title>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/languages/loom.svg" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    <meta name="description" content="Literate programming framework that keeps meaning in your codebase from evaporating. Write your program the way you write a book, with the reasoning in prose and code — as one source." />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
